  "findings": [
    "The hierarchy begins at the real native generated-clock source and preserves both clock sinks and its non-clock feedback/output consumers.",
    "Matching one private branch changes a real emitted repeater delay. Removing that modification yields measurable skew even though settled data values remain correct.",
    "At the deliberately too-fast setting, local observed setup/hold still exceeds the primitive minimum, but the divider feedback value is stale for the intended next raw cycle. Pulse counts and transfer semantics expose this failure.",
    "An asymmetric alternative layout lacks sufficient private-branch delay capacity and is explicitly rejected with a complete rollback. This proof does not establish arbitrary hierarchy clock closure.",
    "The 512 RT raw half-period is a conservative fixture test setting, not an accepted whole-machine clock setting."
  ],
